        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe field/getter/setter, mapper entry, four schema files, OpenAPI doc, and YAML wiring are all textbook-consistent with the sibling `bulkSignupWarning` feature \u2014 additive and idiomatic. The problem is in the one new class, and it's a structural one the codebase has already decided how to handle.\n\n**The city-counting logic is duplicated, against an established centralization idiom in this very codebase.**\n\nLook at the day-based pair:\n- `CheckDailyOwnerLimit` (hard cap) and `AssignBulkSignupWarning` (warning flag) both count owners for a day \u2014 and neither inlines the count. They both call `BusinessDay.countOwnersOn(...)`, whose Javadoc explicitly states it is \"the single definition ... shared by the create-time rules ... so they can never drift apart.\"\n\nNow the city-based pair:\n- `CheckOwnerCityCapacity` (hard cap at 50) counts owners in a city with:\n  ```java\n  ownerRepository.findAll().stream()\n      .map(Owner::getCity)\n      .filter(existing -> existing != null && existing.equalsIgnoreCase(city))\n      .count();\n  ```\n- The new `AssignCityCapacityWarning` (warning flag at 40\u201349) copies that stream **verbatim**.\n\nSo the new code reintroduces exactly the drift risk the `BusinessDay` helper exists to prevent \u2014 the two city rules now carry two independent copies of \"owners in this city, case-insensitively,\" and if one definition changes (e.g. trimming whitespace, handling nulls differently) the cap and the warning silently diverge. The class's own Javadoc even asserts the counts match (\"matching the count used by `CheckOwnerCityCapacity`\"), which is precisely the invariant that should be enforced by shared code, not by a comment.\n\nConcrete fix (mirror the existing idiom):\n- Extract the city count into a single source of truth \u2014 a `CityCapacity` helper analogous to `BusinessDay` (e.g. `static long countOwnersIn(String city, OwnerRepository repository)`), and have both `CheckOwnerCityCapacity` and `AssignCityCapacityWarning` call it.\n- The `CITY_CAPACITY = 50` constant is also now duplicated across the two classes; hang it (and ideally `WARNING_THRESHOLD`) off that shared helper so the hard limit is defined once.\n\nWith that, `AssignCityCapacityWarning` collapses to the same shape as `AssignBulkSignupWarning` (fetch shared count, compare against its own threshold), which is what a maintainer of this codebase would expect to see."
